        "speaking": "Role-play sorting objects with a classmate after class. Open with Qu’est-ce que c’est ? Name an unfamiliar object with C’est un... or C’est une..., then point to a specific known object with Voilà le... or Voilà la.... Ask whether another object is a stylo or a gomme and answer the same question. Close with Merci. Your partner gently checks the article and explains unfamiliar words in English when asked."
      },
      {
        "n": 3,
        "title": "One thing or several: des, les and plurals",
        "objective": "Distinguish one classroom object from several using un/une/des and le/la/les, and hear plural meaning through the article.",
        "focus": "Most French plurals are written with -s: un crayon → des crayons. The final -s is usually silent, so the article often tells you whether one or several are meant: le /lə/ versus les /le/, un or une versus des /de/. Un and une become des for unspecified plural objects; le and la become les for specific plural objects. Nouns already ending in -s, -x, or -z keep that ending in the plural. Other patterns come later; learn each new plural in context.",
